<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel de Amat y Junyent</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1704">1704</a>–<a href="/years/1782">1782</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel de Amat y Junyent was a Catalan soldier who served as governor of Chile from 1755 to 1761 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> from 1761 to 1776. He reorganised the colonial militias after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s losses in the Seven Years' War, carried out the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in 1767 and built public works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>. His liaison with the actress Micaela Villegas, 'La Perricholi', became a famous subject of literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-de-amat-y-junyent.jpg" alt="Manuel de Amat y Junyent" width="320" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pedro Díaz / After Cristóbal Lozano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_D%C3%ADaz_-_Manuel_d%27Amat_i_de_Junyent.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
